// File: t3-azure-nhi-persistence-respond.kql   [STAGING]
//
// Domain: AZURE CONTROL PLANE (Entra audit + Microsoft Graph activity). Reads
// AuditLogs / MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs, keyed on a principal object-id, via
// azure-control-plane/shared-functions.kql. Sibling of the T4 bridges file.
//
// Tactic: PERSISTENCE (TA0003) + the Graph-plane grant that enables PRIVILEGE
// ESCALATION (TA0004). Covers Plane 5 and the NHI-sprawl section of the Sysdig
// TRT incident — see ../references/2026-07-14-sysdig-azure-permission-takeover.md.
//
// TIER: T3 (respond). Higher-volume than the T4 bridges (apps DO get new secrets
// and consented permissions legitimately), so they route to an analyst-actioned
// respond rather than auto-isolate. What makes them T3 and not T2: each is a
// recognised persistence/takeover PRIMITIVE, and the report names the admin
// consent of a tier-0 Graph permission as "itself a high-signal event to alert on."
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS (from the incident): the takeover started with a leaked NHI
// holding one admin-consented Graph permission (RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory)
// and ended with attacker secrets planted on 26 app registrations. There is NO
// native Azure view that lists "every credential on every app registration," so
// the audit-log credential-add event is the reconstructable record of the
// persistence blast radius. Capturing it is the point.
//
// HEADER: GLOBAL (excludeAdmin = true) here — unlike the T4 file. App-lifecycle
// automation (IaC that rotates secrets, app-management pipelines) is exactly the
// benign source, so honoring the KnownAutomationPrincipals allow-list is correct.
// Vet and shorten that list; do NOT allow-list a human-admin identity here.
//
// OPERATOR NOTE: OperationName / permission strings are matched with `contains`
// or `matches regex` (substring), NOT `has`/`has_any` — those are term-indexed
// and miss multi-word phrases, dotted permission names, and hyphenated GUIDs.
//
// FOUR BLOCKS, each its OWN analytics rule:
//   Block 1 (static, AuditLogs) — tier-0 Graph application-permission grant /
//                                 admin consent (the takeover-equivalent event).
//   Block 2 (static, AuditLogs) — credential (secret/cert) added to an app or SP
//                                 (the "26 backdoored apps" persistence primitive).
//   Block 3 (behavior, AuditLogs) — credential-add FAN-OUT: one actor adds
//                                 credentials to N distinct apps in a window.
//   Block 4 (static, MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs) — direct Graph-plane writes to
//                                 app/SP credential endpoints (the plane the
//                                 audit log can miss). OFF BY DEFAULT — see caveat.
//
// COVERAGE CAVEAT: MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs is a tenant diagnostic setting,
// OFF by default, needs Entra P1. In the source incident it started flowing
// mid-attack and MISSED the escalation. Block 4 returns nothing until it's on;
// Blocks 1-3 (AuditLogs) are the durable fallback and should be your primary.


// ============================================================
// Rule: T3-Azure-graph-tier0-permission-grant-respond   (static — AuditLogs)
// The Graph application-permission / admin-consent event. An app-role assignment
// of a tenant-takeover-equivalent Graph permission, or an admin consent that
// includes one, is the capability that made directory takeover possible.
// ============================================================
FilteredAuditLogs(true)   // GLOBAL — honor the automation allow-list
| where Result =~ "success"
| where OperationName in~ (
    "Add app role assignment to service principal",   // application permission granted to an SP
    "Add delegated permission grant",                 // OAuth2 delegated grant
    "Consent to application")                          // admin/user consent flow
| extend _tr = tostring(TargetResources)
// tier-0 Graph permissions (takeover-equivalent) by name, OR the well-known
// appRole GUIDs in case only ids are logged. Substring match on the serialized
// target resources / modified properties.
| where _tr matches regex @"RoleManagement\.ReadWrite\.Directory|AppRoleAssignment\.ReadWrite\.All|Application\.ReadWrite\.All|Directory\.ReadWrite\.All|PrivilegedAccess\.ReadWrite\.AzureADGroup|RoleManagement\.ReadWrite\.Exchange"
    or _tr contains "9e3f62cf-ca93-4989-b6ce-bf83c28f9fe8"   // RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory
    or _tr contains "06b708a9-e830-4db3-a914-8e69da51d44f"   // AppRoleAssignment.ReadWrite.All
    or _tr contains "1bfefb4e-e0b5-418b-a88f-73c46d2cc8e9"   // Application.ReadWrite.All
| extend Severity = "High"
| project TimeGenerated, OperationName, Principal, PrincipalName, ActorIsServicePrincipal,
    TargetResources, Result, CorrelationId
| order by TimeGenerated desc


// ============================================================
// Rule: T3-Azure-app-credential-added-respond   (static — AuditLogs)
// A secret or certificate added to an application registration or service
// principal (T1098.001). The persistence primitive: each added credential is a
// standalone, valid way back in that survives rotating the original leaked secret
// and removing any role grant.
// ============================================================
FilteredAuditLogs(true)   // GLOBAL
| where Result =~ "success"
| extend _tr = tostring(TargetResources)
// credential-management operations (hyphen and en-dash variants both seen), plus
// the broader "Update service principal" gated by the credential filter below.
| where OperationName contains "Certificates and secrets management"
    or OperationName contains "service principal credential"
    or OperationName startswith "Add service principal"
    or OperationName == "Update service principal"
// keep it to credential-bearing changes: `contains "credential"` is case-
// insensitive so it covers passwordCredentials / keyCredentials /
// StrongAuthenticationCredential; KeyDescription catches cert additions.
| where _tr contains "credential" or _tr contains "KeyDescription"
| extend Severity = "High"
| project TimeGenerated, OperationName, Principal, PrincipalName, ActorIsServicePrincipal,
    TargetResources, Result, CorrelationId
| order by TimeGenerated desc


// ============================================================
// Rule: T3-Azure-app-credential-fanout-respond   (behavior — AuditLogs)
// The "26 backdoored app registrations" signature: ONE actor adds credentials to
// MANY distinct apps in a short window. Any single add is Block 2's job; the
// FAN-OUT is the mass-persistence tell and the higher-confidence shape.
// TUNING: threshold (>=5 distinct apps / 1h) is a staging start — set it above
// your busiest legitimate secret-rotation job's per-window app count, or exclude
// that job's SP via KnownAutomationPrincipals. Sentinel scheduled-rule shape: the
// summarize drops per-row ids; map the incident entity on `Principal`.
// ============================================================
let window = 1h;
let threshold = 5;
FilteredAuditLogs(true)   // GLOBAL
| where Result =~ "success"
| extend _tr = tostring(TargetResources)
| where OperationName contains "Certificates and secrets management"
    or OperationName contains "service principal credential"
    or OperationName startswith "Add service principal"
    or OperationName == "Update service principal"
| where _tr contains "credential" or _tr contains "KeyDescription"
// pull the target app/SP identifier so we can count DISTINCT targets per actor
| mv-apply tr = TargetResources on (
    extend TargetAppId = tostring(tr.id), TargetAppName = tostring(tr.displayName)
  )
| summarize
    TargetApps   = make_set(TargetAppName, 100),
    DistinctApps = dcount(TargetAppId),
    Adds         = count(),
    FirstAdd     = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastAdd      = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Principal, PrincipalName, TimeBin = bin(TimeGenerated, window)
| where DistinctApps >= threshold
| extend Severity = "Critical"   // mass credential persistence -> escalate above the single-add T3
| project FirstAdd, LastAdd, Principal, PrincipalName, DistinctApps, Adds, TargetApps, TimeBin
| order by FirstAdd desc


// ============================================================
// Rule: T3-Azure-graph-app-tampering-respond   (static — MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs)
// The Graph-plane view of the same tampering, for what the audit log misses:
// direct writes to application/servicePrincipal credential + appRoleAssignment
// endpoints. OFF BY DEFAULT (see header caveat) — deploy only once the tenant
// diagnostic setting is confirmed flowing; Blocks 1-3 are the primary until then.
// ============================================================
FilteredGraphActivity(true)   // GLOBAL
| where ResponseStatusCode between (200 .. 299)   // successful writes only
| where RequestMethod in~ ("POST", "PATCH", "PUT")
| extend _uri = tolower(tostring(RequestUri))
| where
    // add a password/key credential to an application or service principal
    (_uri contains "/applications/"
        and (_uri contains "addpassword" or _uri contains "addkey"
             or _uri contains "/passwordcredentials" or _uri contains "/keycredentials"))
    or (_uri contains "/serviceprincipals/"
        and (_uri contains "addpassword" or _uri contains "addkey"
             or _uri contains "/passwordcredentials" or _uri contains "/keycredentials"))
    // grant an app-role (application permission) to a service principal
    or (_uri contains "/serviceprincipals/" and _uri contains "approleassignedto")
    or _uri contains "/oauth2permissiongrants"
| extend Severity = "High"
| project TimeGenerated, RequestMethod, RequestUri, Principal, AppId, ResponseStatusCode, IPAddress
| order by TimeGenerated desc


// ============================================================
// NOTES
// - Ownership: the Graph tier-0 PERMISSION grant (Block 1) and the directory-role
//   grant an SP performs USING that permission (T4 bridges file, Block 1) are two
//   different events in the chain — grant-of-capability here, use-of-capability
//   there. Route Block 1 as enrichment onto the T4 chain incident when both fire
//   for the same Principal.
// - The tier-0 permission and appRole-GUID lists are a starting high-risk set:
//   maintain them as your "short, deliberately-maintained" allow-list-by-negation
//   (the report's recommendation #2). Add more AppRoleAssignment.ReadWrite.All
//   friends as your tenant's threat model requires.
